6. The Writ court primarily holding that there was no infirmity or illegality in initiating the proceedings under the Central Act, instead of invoking the provisions of Act 31 of 1978, dismissed the writ petition. Aggrieved by the dismissal of the writ petition and challenging the same, the appellants have preferred the present writ appeal, raising the following grounds:
(a) no Notice u/s.5(a) enquiry was served on the appellants;
(b) No notice u/s.9 and 10 namely award enquiry was served on the appellants;
(c) Even passing of the award was not put on notice of the appellants;
(d) Proceedings under the Central Act were bad and without jurisdiction, in view of the special enacatment viz., of the Tamil Nadu Acquisition of Land of Harijan Welfare Schemes Act 31 of 1978.
(e) Proceedings are barred by limitation;
(f) Section 6 declaration was made after one year from the date of Section 4(1)
notification.
In short, the consistent case of the appeallants before the Writ Court and also before us is that at no point of time, the appellants were served any notice under the Land Acquisition Act and therefore the acquisition proceedings are bad.
